{"questions":[{"question_id":"1lfo01j3u","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2009 Paper 2 Offline","year":2009,"examDate":2009,"subject":"physics","chapter":"simple-harmonic-motion","chapterGroup":"mechanics","topic":"forced-damped-oscillations-and-resonance","type":"mcqm","marks":4,"negMarks":2,"permalink":"pa-student-performed-the-experiment-to-measure-the-speed-o-jee-advanced-physics-motion-18oxik4zdwvlbity","content":"<p>A student performed the experiment to measure the speed of sound in air using resonance air-column method. Two resonances in the air-column were obtained by lowering the water level. The resonance with the shorter air-column is the first resonance and that with the longer air-column is the second resonance. Then,</p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"the intensity of the sound heard at the first resonance was more than that at the second resonance."},{"identifier":"B","content":"the prongs of the tuning fork were kept in a horizontal plane above the resonance tube."},{"identifier":"C","content":"the amplitude of vibration of the ends of the prongs is typically around 1 cm."},{"identifier":"D","content":"the length of the air-column at the first resonance was somewhat shorter than 1/4th of the wavelength of the sound in air."}],"correct_options":["A","D"],"explanation":"<p>The sharpness of resonance decreases with increasing column length.</p>\n<p>The first resonance happens when length of air column <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0.124ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"0.968ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -483 778 428\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2248\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2248\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.581ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1583 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D706\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D706\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(583,0)\"><use data-c=\"2F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2F\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1083,0)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>. The prongs of the tuning fork are always kept in a vertical plane and amplitude of vibration is typically in the mm range.</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1lft4bha4/1c05367a-b934-42b2-9763-bf2c011e070e/7b3da7c0-cde0-11ed-9ce1-7bd2ccf24231/file-1lft4bha5.png?format=png\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/1lft4bha4/1c05367a-b934-42b2-9763-bf2c011e070e/7b3da7c0-cde0-11ed-9ce1-7bd2ccf24231/file-1lft4bha5.png\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0px auto;max-height:40vh\" alt=\"IIT-JEE 2009 Paper 2 Offline Physics - Simple Harmonic Motion Question 9 English Explanation\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/1lft4bha4/1c05367a-b934-42b2-9763-bf2c011e070e/7b3da7c0-cde0-11ed-9ce1-7bd2ccf24231/file-1lft4bha5.png?format=png 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/1lft4bha4/1c05367a-b934-42b2-9763-bf2c011e070e/7b3da7c0-cde0-11ed-9ce1-7bd2ccf24231/file-1lft4bha5.png?format=png 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1lft4bha4/1c05367a-b934-42b2-9763-bf2c011e070e/7b3da7c0-cde0-11ed-9ce1-7bd2ccf24231/file-1lft4bha5.png?format=png 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/1lft4bha4/1c05367a-b934-42b2-9763-bf2